title: Holding a repository in memory
tags: Storage Repository
kind: guide
aliases: in-memory in_memory resident memory-dir tmpfs
summary: Loaded with --in-memory, a repository's store is copied into RAM (a tmpfs) and read there, so that no read waits on the disk; every write is committed to the disk first, then to the copy.

```
mf repo load --in-memory <root>
```

or ''Load in memory'' in the [[repos panel]]'s load form, `repos:load in-memory` from
the keyboard (and `repos:toggle-memory` for one already loaded, below), `{"root": …, "in_memory": true}` for [[POST /repos/load]] — and, for a
repository the daemon loads at startup, `in-memory = true` in its `[[load]]` entry
([[Daemon configuration]]).

The load copies the store into a directory in RAM — its phase `memory` counts the
bytes — then the repository works as any other, reading the copy:

* ''Reads'' find every page in RAM: none waits on the disk, not the first query after a boot, nor one whose pages the kernel would have evicted. The copy lives in a `tmpfs`, whose pages are never dropped (only swapped, when the machine has swap and runs out of RAM).
* ''Writes'' are made on the copy, where what they read is in RAM too; the keys a write puts and deletes are then replayed on the store on disk and committed there, with its `fsync`, and only then is the copy committed. The disk stays the store a crash leaves, and a crash loses nothing that was answered.
* The watcher, the tasks, the log: as usual. Unloading removes the copy.

The copies go to `/dev/shm/metafolder-<uid>/` — `memory-dir` in the daemon's
`[settings]` ([[Daemon configuration]]) — one directory per repository, which takes the
store's size in RAM for as long as it is held: hold in memory only what fits. A store
larger than the free room there is refused, at the load or at the switch. A daemon
starting removes the copies a crash left: each holds a lock while in use, so another
daemon's copies stay.

`GET /repos` lists, for a repository held so, `in_memory: {bytes}`, the size of its
copy — `null` for one loaded the ordinary way.

!! When the disk or the memory refuses a write

* ''The disk refuses it'' (full, failing): the write is undone in the copy too, and answered with its error, as for an ordinary load. Nothing happened.
* ''The copy refuses it after the disk took it'' — the memory is full: the copy is behind the disk for good. The repository is taken out of service and unloaded, listed as lost with that reason (`lost_reason` in `GET /repos?lost=true`, the [[repos panel]]'s row) as a repository whose root went away is ([[When the root moves]]); every request to it answers `503` with the reason. Nothing on disk is lost: loading it again reads the disk. A full memory is a fault of the machine, not one the daemon works around (user decision, October 2026).

!! Switching a loaded repository

A loaded repository is held in memory, or let go of, without unloading it:
`repos:toggle-memory [repo]` (the active repository by default) from the keyboard, or
`{"in_memory": true}` / `false` for [[POST /repos/:repo/memory]]. Held, the store is
copied in as at a load — and, for that while, every other request to the repository
waits, as it would for the load's `memory` phase. Let go, the copy is removed and the
store is read on disk again. Asked for what already is, nothing is done.

`POST /repos/load` with `in_memory` on a repository already loaded the ordinary way is
refused (`409`): a load of a loaded repository does nothing, so it would copy nothing
in — the error names the switch above. Loading in memory a repository already held in
memory is the ordinary idempotent load.

!! What it costs, what it buys

The load copies the whole store, at the speed of reading it from the disk; the
memory is the store's size, for as long as it is held. Each write is written twice:
computed once, on the copy, then replayed key by key on the disk — no derived index is
computed again — and a write to the disk may still read the pages of the disk's B-tree
it changes. Writes are no faster than an ordinary load's; reads are, wherever the
ordinary load would have read from the disk.

!! Why a copy, and not the store locked in memory

The first version (October 2026) read the store's pages in at load and locked them
there with `mlock`. A process may lock only so much — the memory-lock limit, 8 MiB for
a user by default — and raising it for the daemon is a system setting no one should
need: a real store was never locked, only read in, and the kernel evicted it again
soon after. A copy in a `tmpfs` takes no lock and has no such limit (user decision,
October 2026).

Two copies are kept in step by replaying each write's own changes, not by applying the
write twice: the keys written are the same bytes on both sides by construction, where
two runs of the same write could allocate different ids. Committing to the disk first
means a write either is on the disk, or did not happen.

This answers, on demand, the cold-cache question of [[Storage open questions]]: an
ordinary load still reads nothing at open.
